Members of the Wales Assembly who are worried about “spoiling puppets”
Welsh officials are appalled by a proposal to replace their title by the abbreviation MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
This proposal stems from the draft amendment to the name of the assembly, which would become the Welsh Parliament.
In the political sphere, members of the Assembly believe that this could be the subject of mockery.
An al-Liberian politician indicated that his group was concerned that the acronym "smages with Twp and Pwp".
For readers not originating in Wales: in Welsh, twp means stupid, and pwp means crotte.
According to one of its members, Plaid Cymru (Wife Party) was not “satisfied” and proposed other options.
The conservative party would be “extensive” to a name change, but pointed out that between MWP and Muppet (martinette), the distance is narrow.
In this context in Welsh, the letter w is pronunciated as the letter u in English from the Yorkshire region.
The Committee of the Assemblie, which is currently preparing legislation introducing the change of name, has stated that “the final decision concerning the naming of the members of the Assembly rests with them”.
The Government of Wales (Government of Wales Act of 2017) gives the Welsh Assembly the authority to change its name.
In the month of June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposal to designate the Assembly as the Welsh Parliament, which was widely supported.
On the issue of members' title, the Commission is leaning in favour members of the Welsh Parliament (WMP); however, a vast majority of Welwians prefer MWP.
It seemed that the members of the Assembly made other suggestions, but the effort to reach a consensus could prove to be a veritable headache for Elin Jones, the Speaker of the Assembly, who is expected to submit a bill on the change of name in the coming weeks.
The legislation on the reforms will include further changes concerning the functioning of the assembly, in particular the rules on the exclusion of members and the design of the committee system.
The members of the General Assembly will take a final decision on the question of the name during the discussions on the bill.
Macedonia: referendum on the modification of the country's name
Macedonians will decide on Sunday whether their country should henceforth bear the name “Republic of North Macedonia”.
The popular vote was organised in an attempt to resolve decades-old dispute between the country and its Greek neighbour, for which Macedonia is the name of one of its provinces.
For a very long time, Athens has been insisting that the name of Macedonia constitutes a demand on its territory and has repeatedly vetoed the accession of its neighbouring country to the European Union and NATO.
The President of the People’s Republic of Macedonia, Gjorge Ivanov, opposed to the referendum, indicated that he would not take the results into account.
However, proponents such as Prime Minister zoran zaev maintain that the change of name is only a small price to pay for joining the EU and NATO.
