Before being driven back by skyrocket-and-riot police, hidden separatists threw breezes and coloured powder, creating dust clouds in the streets, which do not usually decelerate tourists.
Scupons also erupted later in the course of the day, while the police used truncheons to contain the clashes.
For several hobbies, independence groups chanting “no forgetfulness or forgiveness,” faced unionist supporters screaming “long-lives for Spain.”
Forty-seven protesters with minor injuries were treated, according to the local press.
One year after the referendum of 1 October, deemed illegal by Madrid but celebrated by the Catalans in favour of separation, tensions are still lenient in the pro-introversial region.
An overwhelming majority of electors chose independence, despite a low turn-entancy, with opponents of seceasing largely complicating the referendum.
The Catalan authorities, nearly 1 000 people were injured last year after the police tried to prevent Catalans from entering polling stations in the region.
Pro-introversist groups camped on Friday night to block a demonstration in support of the National Police.
It still took place, but was forced to take a different itinerary.
Narcis Termes, a 68-year-old electrician who competed with his wife in the separatist demonstration, stated that he no longer had any hope for Catalonia’s independence.
“Lifted a long time last year.
My parents wriphamate with joy at the idea of being able to vote, but now we are at an impasse,” he laments.
In spite of their crucial, albeit slim, victory in the regional elections last December, Catalan pro-independence parties have found it difficult to maintain momentum this year, with many popular leaders exalted or delinquiled pending their being on trial for organizing the referendum and declaring Catalonia’s independence.
42-year-year-old mechanic Joan Puig, who is filming the demonstration in support of the police on his phone, believes that politicians are responsible for blocking the situation on both sides.
“Futlegrancy only rises,” he laments.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders in prison awaiting a judgment since last year, announced his intention to present his candidacy in the European Parliament elections next year.
“This is for me the best way to denunciate the regression of democratic values and unleashed repression by the Spanish government,” he adds.
