Miscellaneous Home by President Erdogan in Cologne
On Saturday, 29 September, the Turkish and German leaders met for breakfast in Berlin in a relaxed atmosphere.
This is the last day of President Erdogan’s controversial visit to Germany, which aimed to improve relations between NATO’s allies.
Various issues were addressed, such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey’s accession to the EU.
Erdogan then flew to Cologne for the inauguration of one of the largest mosques.
One of the most important Turkish communities abroad lives in this city.
Citing security reasons, the police prevented 25,000 protesters from rallying outside the edifice, while many supporters were able to get closer to see their president.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters, mostly Kurd, made their voices heard, condemning both Erdogan’s policies and the German government’s decision to welcome him into his country.
The cleavage that emerges from the protests reflects Erdogan’s dual vision: a visitor greeted as a hero by some Germans of Turkish origin and described as self-grid by others.
