On Saturday, the authorities stated that at least 384 people had perished, mostly sweeping by huge waves on the beaches while an earthquake, followed by a zimunvent, beating the Indonesian island of Celebes.
On Friday, hundreds of people gathered for a festival on the beach in Palu, when waves six metres high submerged the beach at nightfall; they took many lives and destroyed everything in their path.
The 7.5-magnitude earthquake preceded the Tsunami.
“When the Tsunami threat sprinkled yesterday, everyone was still on the beach. People didn’t shuffle immediately, it took their lives,” said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho Spokesperson for the National Catastrophic Management Agency (BNPB), at a press conference in Jakarta.
“Tsunami didn’t happen lonely. It dragged cars, pieces of wood, homes, it took everything with it,” explained Nugroho, adding that the Tsunami moved into the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before hitting the shore.
Some people were able to save their lives by climbing the trees, he adds.
Nearly 16,700 people were evacueed towards 24 centres in Palu.
Aerial images published by the Disaster Management Agency show destroyed buildings and shops, deformed and collapsed bridges, as well as a mosque surrounded by water.
Replicas kept shaking the coastal city on Saturday.
Earthquakes were felt by 2.4 million people.
The Indonesian Technology Evaluation and Application Agency (BPPT) has stated that the energy unleashed by Friday’s Earthquake is 200 times greater than the atomic bomb leased on Hiroshima during World War II during World War II.
