Bodies were found trapped under the rubble of ruined buildings, he added, adding that 540 people were injured and 29 were still missing.
Nugroho stated that human and material losses could be greater over the 300-km coast north of Palu, an area called Donggala, closest to the epicenter of the Earthquake.
The communications were “completely paralyzed” since Donggala, Nugroho said.
More than 300,000 people are living there,” The Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its staff and volunteers were moving to the affected areas.
“The situation is dramatic and could well get worse,” she added.
On Saturday, the agency was widely criticized for failing to disseminate the news that a tsunami had touched Palu, although agents stated that the waves had arrived while the warning had been published.
An amateur video shared on social networks shows a man on the floor of a building frantically screeching to passers-by in the street that a tsunami is approaching.
A few minutes later, a wall of water falls on the coast, taking with it buildings and cars.
Reuters could not authenticate the images immediately.
Earthquakes and the tsunami caused a general power outage that cut all communications in Palu, making it difficult for the authorities to co-ordinate rescue operations.
The military has begun to deliver aid via cargo planes from Jakarta and other cities, according to the authorities, but evacuees are still in great need for food and other basic necessities.
The city airport has re-exercised as part of rescue operations, but will remain closed until October.
