Kanye West: rapper changes his name and becomes Ye
Rapper Kanye West is now called Ye.
Announcing the tweeping story on Saturday, he wrote, "formerly known as Kanye West."
At the age of 41, West, who has been called Ye for some time, used this nickname as a title for his eighth album, released in June.
The rapper made this change just before arriving on Saturday Night Live, where he is expected to present his new album, Yandhi.
His participation replaces Ariana Grande, who canceled his arrival for "emotional reasons," the show’s creator explained.
In addition to being the abbreviation of its current peer-to-peer, this nickname also has religious significance for West.
"I think 'Ye' is the most used word in the Bible, where it means 'you", explained West earlier this year, evoking the title of his album with its host Big Boy.
"Then I'm you, I'm us.
I moved from Kanye, which means "the only one," to Ye -- the reflection of good, of evil, of confusion, of everything.
This album is a reflection of who we are."
West is not the only rapper known to change his name.
Sean Combs called Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year announced his preference for Love and Brother Love.
JAY, which has collaborated with West in the past, has also played hyphen and capital letters.
In Mexico, AMLO stated that it would never use military force against civilians.
Mexican elected President Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador promised never to use military force against civilians, almost fifty years after bloody reprisals by the security forces against students.
