The United States Should Dollars in Artificial Investigation
While China is increasingly active in the field of artificial intelligence, the United States is expected to double its funding for research in this sector, according to artificial intelligence Investor and Expert Kai-Fu Lee, who worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
These statements come after several U.S. U.Scientun AI admonition, while an official AI strategy in the United States is lacking.
At the same time, China unleashed its roadmap last year: it plans to become the leader in AI innovation by 2030.
“Duplicating funds for AI research would be a good start, because all the other countries are one step ahead of the United States, and we’re looking for the next great AI discovery,” Lee explains.
Dupunciation of funds could double the chances that the next key step in the area of AI came from the United States, Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published earlier this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of China’s most important IAR companies, Face.
In the 1980s at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an AI system that beat the highest-ranked American Othello player. He then joined Microsoft Reasearch’s management team and became president of Google’s Chinese branch.
Lee had previously participated in technology competitions organized by the U.S. government, such as the Robotics The Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency’s Robotics Challenge, and asked when the next competition would take place, in order to identify visionaries.
According to Lee, U.S. researchers often have to work hard to obtain funding from the government.
“It is not China that owns academic leaders, it is the companies,” says Lee.
In recent years, Facebook, Google, and other tech companies have recruited leading academic names to work on AI.
Lee added that changes in immigration policies could also support US AI efforts.
'I think green cards should automatically be granted to AI researchers,'” he says.
China’s State Council unveiled next-generation artificial intelligence development plan in July 2017.
The National Natural Sciences Foundation of China provides funding to members of academic institutions, just like the National Science Foundation and other government organizations distribute money to American researchers, but the quality of academic work in China is less,” Lee explained.
Earlier this year, the United States Department of Defense established a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, which must involve partners from the industry and the academy, when the White House announced the formation of a Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
Earlier this month, DARPA announced an investment of 2 Billion Dollars in an initiative called AI Next.
NSF, for its part, currently invests more than USD 100 million a year in AI research.
At the same time, U.S. legislation to establish a National Security Commission on Artificial Imquipment has taken no action for several months.
Macedonians take part in the referendum on the name of their country
On Sunday, the inhabitants of Macedonia made their voices heard in a referendum on the change of their country’s name to the Republic of North Macedonia, which would resolve a decades-old dispute with Greece, which had stalled Macedonia’s application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, of which one of the provinces is called Macedonia, maintains that the name of its northern neighbour represents a right on its territory and vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments signed an agreement based on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents claim that the change would affect the ethical identity of Macedonia’s majority Sla Slavic population.
President Gjorge Ivanov announced that he would not vote in the referendum and that a boycott campaign had raised doubts about obtaining a minimum of 50 per cent participation required to validate the referendum.
The question of the referendum was: “Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU under the terms of the agreement signed with Greece?”
According to supporters of the name-changing, including Prime Minister 'oran 'aev, this is the price of joining organisations such as the EU and 'NATO for Macedonia, who are rescued from the collapse of Yugoslavia.
“I have come today to vote for the future of my country, for the youth of Macedonia, so that it can live freely under the aegis of the European Union. This means a safer life for all of us,” said Olivera Georgijevska, aged 79, in Skopje.
Although the vote is not legally binding, enough members of parliament have stated that they would follow the result of the vote to make it decisive.
Changing the name would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission announced that no irregularity had been detected at 1 p.m.
But voter turnout was only 16%, from 34% at 34% at the same time in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, which mobilized 66% of registered voters.
“I came to vote for my children. Our place is in Europe,” says 62-year-old Gjose Tanevski, who voted in the capital, Skopje.
The Prime Minister of the Democratic Republic of the United States of America and his son Dushko took part in the referendum and voted in favour of changing the name to join NATO and the European Union in Strumica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018.
Vladimir Kavardarkov, a 54-year-old parliament in Skopje is preparing a small stage and has seats in front of tempts set up by those who will boycott the referenda.
“We want to join the EU and NATO, but we want to enter through the big gate,” said Kavadarkov.
“We are a poor country, but we have our dignity.
If they don’t want from us as Macedonia, we can turn to China and Russia and participate in Eurasian integration.”
According to Prime Minister zaev, NATO membership will enable Macedonia to benefit from the necessary investment, and the unemployment rate in the country exceeds 20 per cent.
“I believe that a large majority will be in favour, because more than 80% of our citizens want to join the EU and NATO,” zaev said after filing his ballot.
He added that a “yes” would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a survey published on Monday by the Institute for Policy Research in Macedonia, between 3 and 43 per cent of voters intend to take part in the referendum, which is lower than the expected turn-operating.
Another survey, organised by the Macedonian channel Telma, estimated the turnout at 57%.
Seventy-seven per cent of those intending to vote stated that they would vote “yes”.
For the referendum to be valid, 50 per cent of the votes plus a vote is required.
Failing the referendum would be the first step toward pro-Western government’s policy since taking power in May last year.
