The Labour Party is no longer the big community it was.
It is not tolerant of those who express their ideas
When Activists from the Momentum movement in my local party voted to censor me, it was no surprise.
After all, I am the last in a line of Labour Party parliamentarians who are considered not to be welcome, all for having expressed our ideas.
My parliamentarian colleague Joan Ryan received the same treatment because she rose against anti-semitism.
As far as I am concerned, I was censored because I disagreed with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy on national security over Europe. Ironically, these are subjects for which Jeremy himself disagreed with previous leaders.
Notifying the Nottingham East Labour rally on Friday said: “We want the rally to be inclusive and productive.”
This is exactly what Friday Night General Assemblies were during my eight years as a Labour MP.
Unfortunately, this is not the case for many rallies today, and the promise of gentle and more respectful policies has been forgotten for a long time if it has ever been taken into account.
It has become clear that opposite opinions are no longer tolerated within the party; each opinion is judged according to its acceptability by party leaders.
It started shortly after Jeremy took power. Colleagues with whom I intended to share the same political vision began to expect me to turn my jacket back and take positions at the opposite of my convictions, whether on national security or on the EU’s internal market.
Every time I talked publicly, and no matter what I was saying, followed an abusive tirade on social networks, calling for withdrawal, denunvoking center policies, telling me that I shouldn't be part of the party.
And that's not just my experience.
By the way, I am luckily than some of my colleagues, because the comments addressed to me were of a political nature.
I admire the professionalism and the determination of these colleagues, who face a surge of sexist or racist words every day, but who do not give up.
One of the most unfavorable things in today’s policy is the standardisation of different forms of abuse.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn stated that the Labour Party must encourage a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer this great community, and that the party is shrinking through no-confidence motions and changing selection rules.
I have been given a lot of advice in the last couple of years: look down, don’t express my ideas so loud, and everything would go well.
But it is not to act in this way that I do politics.
I rejoined the Labour Party 32 years ago as a run-away student, because the negligence of Thatcher’s government caused the collapsing of my classroom. Since then, I've wanted to work for better public services for those who need it most, whether as a local councillor or a government minister.
I have never concealed my political ambitions, even in the last elections.
No one at Nottingham East could be disrupted by my political positions and my differences with current party leaders.
All I would like to say to those who defended the motion on Friday is that the country is moving towards a Brexit that will affect homes, businesses, and public services. I don’t think this is really the time to lose time and energy by debating my loyalty to the party leader.
My only message is not for Nottingham Momentum, it is for my constituents, whether they are Labour or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise you that no threat of withdrawal or expedition will prevent me from acting on the causes that I stand for, which are your common interests.
Chris Leslie is a parliamentarian for Nottingham East.
