Absolutely throughout Bolivia, the remembrance of a rib lost to the benefit of Chile during a bloody resource conflict in the 19th-century is still there, as is the desire to sail on the Pacific Ocean, once again.
These hopes have not been so palpable for several decades; indeed, Bolivia awaits a decision of the International Court of Justice, which will be delivered on 1 October, after five years of deliberation.
“Bolivia has the will, a spirit of unity and serenity, and of course expects a positive result,” explains Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will follow the decision of the International Court of Court of Public Justice throughout the country throughout the country, hoping that the Court in The The The Hague will speak in favour of the Bolivian request; Bolivia hopes that, after decades of sporadic negotiations, Chile will be obliged to negotiate the agreement of a sovereign transition to the ocean to Bolivia.
