“We are so close to returning to the Pacific Ocean,” he said at the end of August.
But some anablishers believe that there is little chance that the court will decide in favour of Chile, and that little would change, even in the event of a positive decision.
The United Nations body based in the Netherlands did not have the power to grant Chilean territory, and stipulated that it would not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the International Court of International Court of Justice renders its decision six months after the latest arguments shows that the case “was uncomplicated,” explains Chilean international law expert Paz zrate.
Far from favouring the cause of Bolivia, the last four years may have even harmed it.
“The problem of access to the sea has been misguided by the current Bolivian administration,” he said.
Morales belligerent rhetoric has undermined all the Chilean good will that remained, she suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to interact, but after that, it will be very complicated to hold talks.
The last exchange of ambassadors between the two countries took place in 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodriguez Veltzé, representing Bolivia in The The Hague, rejected the idea that the Court’s decision was an unabsolutely rapid.
Monday will give Bolivia “an extraordinary opportunity to start a new era of relations with Chile” and an opportunity to “put an end 139 years of disagreement with mutual benefits,” he said.
Calzadilla also disputed that Morales – which to this day remains one of the most popular presidents in Latin America – used the maritime problem as a political crutch.
