John Edward: Language proficiency is essential to the citizens of the world
Independent schools in Scotia are maintaining an issue of academic excellence, which has continued in 2018, with new and excellent examination results reinforced by collective and individual success in sports, art, music and other community activities.
With more than 30,000 pupils throughout Scotland, these schools represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) strive to provide the highest level of service to students and their parents.
Self-reliant schools are working to prepare their pupils for higher education, for the careers they have chosen and in their place as citizens of the world.
As an education sector capable of designing and setting up a tailor-made school curriculum, we see that contemporary languages remain popular and desired subjects in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you speak to a man in a language he understands, you speak at the helhead.
If you talk to him in his language, you speak to his heart."
It reminds us that we cannot rely on English to create relations and build trust with people from other countries.
If you look at the excellent results of the exams this year, we can see that languages are at the top of the ranking, with the most important success rates in independent schools.
In total, 68 per cent of the students who studied foreign languages scored the highest.
The data, collected from the SCIS schools, show that 72% of the pupils received the highest score, A in Mandarin, as did 72% of students studying German, 69% of pupils who studied French and the 63 per cent of pupils who studied Spanish.
This shows that independent schools in Scotia support foreign languages as absolutely essential skills for children and young people in the future.
From now on, languages as a subject are organized as science, technology, engineering and mathematics in the curricula of self-selected schools and other schools.
A UK Employment and Competency Survey in 2014 reveals that 17% of the difficulties faced by employers in recruiting applicants are related to inadequate language proficiency.
Language proficiency is therefore becoming increasingly vital in preparing young people for their future careers.
With more job offers requiring languages, these competences are essential in our globalised world.
Regardless of their choice of quarry, someone who speaks a second language will have a real advantage in the future thanks to this sustainable skill.
Being able to communicate directly with strangers will automatically place the multilingual person vis-à-vis its competitors.
According to a YouGov survey conducted in 2013 among 4000 adults living in the United Kingdom, 75 per cent of them did not have enough control of a foreign language to hold a conversation.
This is why investing in language learning is important for children today.
By locating several languages, especially those of emerging economies, children will have a better chance of finding rewarding jobs.
In Scotia, each school will teach different languages.
Several of them will focus on classic contemporary languages, while others will teach languages considered important for the United Kingdom for 2020, including Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of the preferences of your child, several languages will be accessible in independent schools, with specialist staff in the field.
Independent Scottish schools want to provide a learning environment that will prepare children and give them the skills they need to succeed, no matter what the future holds for them.
It is now clear that, in a global trading environment, languages remain of paramount importance for the country’s future, and this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages should be regarded as “competence of international communication”.
Self-receivable schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to Scottish youth.
You have to do it.
John Edward is the Managing Director of the Scottish Council of Independent
