North Korea declares that “non-confidous” disarmament will “non-confident” take place unilaterally without confidence.
On Saturday, the North Korean Foreign Minister told the United Nations that the continuation of the sanctions reinforced his distrust towards the United States and that it would be impossible for his country to unilaterally renounce its nuclear weapons in these circumstances.
At the annual General Assembly of the Global Agency, Ri Yong Ho stated that North Korea last year took “important goodwill measures,” such as putting an end such as the cessation of nuclear and missile tests, the dismantling of the nuclear-test site and its commitment not to proliferate nuclear weapons and nuclear-technology.
'However, we don't see a corresponding response from the United States," he lagged
In the absence of trust in the United States, we are pampering for our national security and, in such circumstances, it is out of the exclusion that we are the first to disarm ourselves unilaterally.”
While repeating North-Korea’s well-known reproaches about Washington’s opposition to a “participal” approach to denuclearization under which North Korea would be re-energized if it gradually took action, its declaration proved important in that it does not reject the idea of unilateral denuclearization as Pyongyang did in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump, made on June 12 in Singapore at the very first summit between a current US president and a North Korean ruler, when Kim pledged to work toward “denuclearization of the Korean peninsula,” while Trump promised to guarantee North Korea’s security.
North Korea is seeking to officially end the Korean War (1950-1953), but the United States has stated that Pyongyang must first renounce its nuclear weapons.
D.S.C. has also rejected demands to relax harsh international sanctions against North Korea.
the United States insist that North Korea first denuclearize and accentuate the pressure by imposing sanctions to achieve its goal coercive, even opposing the “declaration of the end-of-morning war.”
“Those who believe that these sanctions can make us ployer don't know us.
The problem is that maintaining sanctions increases our distrust.”
Ri made no comment on the second summit between Kim and Trump highlighted before the United Nations by the American leader earlier this week.
Instead, the minister highlighted three meetings between Kim and South Korean ruler Moon Jae-in over the past five months, adding: “While the party dealing with denuclearization was South Korea and not the United States, the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not be at such an impasse.”
Nevertheless, the tone of Ri’s speech was radically different from that of last year, when he told the United United Nations General Assembly that it was inevitable that North Korea’s missiles could be pointed at North American territory after “wrong” President Trump described Kim as a “missile man” on a suicide mission.
Trump, who last year threatened before the United Nations to “totally destroy” North-Kora North Korea auvelated this year on the courage with which Kim applied disarmament measures, but added that there was still a lot of work to be done and that the sanctions would not be lifted until North Korea disarmed.
On Wednesday, Trump said that no deadline had been set. “It can take two years, three years, or five months; it doesn’t matter,” he said.
China and Russia argue that the United Nations Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures adopted.
However, US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the United Nations Security Council last Thursday that: “The application of Security Council sanctions must be firmly and systematically maintained until complete, permanent and verified denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously tightened sanctions against North Korea since 2006 to stifle the financing of Pyongyang’s nuclear programs and ballistic missiles.
Pompeo met Ri on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assembly and subsequently announced that he would return to Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit.
