Harvest students take relaxation classes
This year, Harvard University has set up a new course that allows all undergraduate students to sleep more and combat the growing machist trend of consuming caffeine throughout the night.
A professor noticed that most students at the best university in the world don't know how to take care of them
Designed by Charles Czeisler, Professor of Sleeping Medicine at the Harvard Medical School and Specialist at Brigham and Women’s Hospital, is the first of its kind in the United States.
It is after giving a lecture on the effects of sleep deprivation on learning that the idea came to him
By the end, a student approached and said, "Why do they tell me only in the final year?"
She claimed that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep, which surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
Offered for the first time this year, this course explains to students the need to adopt good sleeping habits in order to improve their academic and physical performance, as well as their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, psychiatry professor at the Harvard Medical School and director-general of health services at the university, stated that the university had decided to introduce the course after finding that the students were suffering from a severe lack of sleep during the week.
Lecture time consists of various interactive exercises.
One of the sections presents the image of a dormitory containing different objects (coffee cups, curtains, sneakers, books...) on which the students click to know the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the impact of lack of sleep on their physical performances and the importance of integrating a routine before bed.
In another section, participants learn how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
A campus map with interactive icons then invites participants to reflect on their daily routine.
“We know that this willn’t instantaneously change student behavior.
However, in our opinion, they have the right to know this information - in the same way that you have the right to know the effects of smoking on health", added Professor Czeisler.
In his view, the pride culture of “suffering a white night” still exists, and he adds that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure exerted on the students are causing a growing sleep shortage.
Sufficient sleep, of good quality, should be the “secret weapon” of students to combat stress, exhaustion, and anxiety,” he said – even to avoid gaining weight, because lack of sleep puts the brain in starving mode, making them permanently starving.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old Califorian chemist chemist and physical biology student, helped Professor Czeisler conceive the course after serving as one of his students last year at Harvard.
He stated that the course had opened his eyes and forced him to insist that it be offered throughout the campus.
It hopes that the next step will be to offer all graduate students to follow a similar curriculum before rejoining the reputable institution.
Prof. Czeisler advises students to provide an alarm for both bedtime and wawake-wake-wakewave, and to be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” emitted by electronic screens and LED light, which can disrupt their circadian rhythm and lead to stubbling.
