Almanach: Inventor Geiger
And right now, a page from our "Sunday Morning" Almanac: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today, and THE TOURCY CUTTER... was born the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Han" Geiger in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity; an invention that subsequently spawned the device known as Geiger counter.
A pillar of science since then, Geiger has also become fundamental in pop culture, as can be seen in the film "Bells of Coronado" of 1950, with Roy Rogers and Dale Evans, two unlikely scientists:
"What is this?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger meter, we use it to look for radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
Thanks to these earphones, we can really hear the effects of atoms released into minerals through radioactivity.”
Evans: "Tell me, it's for sure it is jumping there."
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name remains.
A new cancer vaccine enables the immune system to “detect” cancer cells
A new cancer vaccine allows the Immune System to “defensify” cancer cells and kill them
As part of the treatment, the vaccine tells the immune system how to recognise cancer cells
This technique consists in extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the lab.
They can then “dissure” a protein that is common to many cancers, and are then reinjected
An experimental vaccine shows encouraging results in patients with different types of cancer.
A woman who received the vaccine, whose purpose is to teach the immune system to recognise the cancer cells, saw her ovarian cancer go away for more than 18 months.
The method involves extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the lab, so that they can detect the HER2 protein, common to many cancers, and finally re-insert them.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the American National Cancer Institute based in Bethesda, Maryland, said: “Our results indicate that we have a very promising vaccine.”
According to Professor Berzofsky, the protein HER2 “induces an increase in several types of cancer”, including breast, ovarian, pulmonary and colorectal cancer.
A similar approach of taking immune cells to “learn” them about targeting cancer cells has been proven in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
