HIV/SAIDS: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China reported an increase of 14 per cent in its inhabitants living with HIV/AIDS.
According to the Chinese health authorities, more than 820,000 people are involved.
Nearly 4 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
Most of the patients have been infected during sexual intercourse, which is a new development.
Historically, the virus spread rapidly in some parts of the country as a result of contaminated blood transfusions.
However, the number of HIV-contaminated people in this way has been virtually shrugged to zero, Chinese health authorities said at a conference held in Yunnan province.
However, from one year to the next, the number of people living with HIV/AIDS in China increased by 100 000.
In the Chinese LGBT community, the transmission of HIV through sexual relations is a serious problem.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against the LGBT community remains widespread.
Due to the conservative values of the country, studies estimate that 70 to 90 per cent of gay men end up marrying a woman.
Many cases of transmission are due to inadequate sexual protection.
As part of its efforts to combat this problem, the Chinese government has, since 2003, spawned universal access to anti-HIV drugs.
Maxine Waters laments that a GOP employee leapt information about senators, and denunciates “dangerous lyrias” and “conspiracy theories.”
On Saturday, U.S. Republican Maxine Waters denounced allegations that a member of her team had published personal information about three Republican senators on legislator Wikipedia.
The Los Angeles Democrat claimed that these statements were supported by “exfar-of-the-right” experts.
“Lies, lyrics, and still infamous lies,” Waters reacted in a tweeted statement.
