What is the Global Citizen Festival and did anything to reduce poverty?
On this Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual musical event featuring an all-improving list of celebrities and an equally impressive mission: ending poverty in the world.
The Global Citizen Festival, now in its seventh edition, will see tens of thrift to the Great Lawn of Central Park in order not only to take advantage of the performance of artists such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise public awareness of the real goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, whose first edition took place in 2012, is an extension Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to eradicate poverty by increasing the number of people who actively combat it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you are willing to pay for a VIP ticket), spectators had to perform a series of tasks, or “actions,” such as volunteering, sending an e-mail e-mail to a global leader, calling or making any other useful effort to raise public awareness about the fight against poverty.
But to what extent can Global Citizen still achieve its goal in the remaining twelve years?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free gracious way to persuade them to demand a call for action, or is it just another example of “clickivism” – a situation in which people really think to make a difference by signing a petition or tweeting a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has registered more than 19 million “shares” by its supporters, who argue in favour of many different goals.
According to the organization, these actions have helped to encourage world leaders to make commitments and adopt policies amounting to more than 37-billion-dollar that are expected to change the lives of more than USD 2.25 billion by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group mentioned 390 commitments and announcements arising from its actions, of which a tranche of at least USD 10 billion has already been disburdened.
The group estimates that guaranteed funds have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a British partnership of investors and execuluders committed to “help children develop their full potential,” while pamphaging Sterling Pound35 Million Pound in Rwanda to End Mal Mal Mal Mal Mal Malnutrition After Receiving more than 4,700 messages from Global Citizens.
“Thanks to the support of the British Government, donors, national governments and Global Citizens like you, we can make the social injustice of undernourishment a footnote in history,” Tracey Oullman Camericulum, The Power of Nutrition Ambassador, told Camera at a concert held in London in April 2018.
The group has also stated that following the company of more than 5,000 actions aimed at asking the United Kingdom to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced the financing of a project, The Power of Nutrition, which will benefit 5 million women and children as part of nutritional interventions.
In response to one of the most Frequently Asked Questions on its website, “what makes you think it is possible to put an end to Extreme Poverty?”
Global Citizen said: “The road to it will be long and difficult, sometimes we will fall and fail.
However, like the major civil rights and anti-parthetil apartheid movements, we will get there, because we are more powerful together.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Mone are among the artists who will perform this year in New York, where Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman will host the show.
