Inventor of the World Wide Web Considers Launching a New Internet to Defy Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching an entirely new company that seeks to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of the tech legend, Inrupt, is a company that takes advantage of Berners-Lee's Free Solid platform.
Solid makes it possible for users to choose where their data is saved as well as the persons authorized to access specific information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jokingly stated that Inrupt aimed to “dominate the world.”
“We have to do it right now,” he said about the launch.
“This is a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid technology to enable people to create their own “on-line personal data store” or DPL.
It can contain contact lists, task lists, a calendar, a music library as well as other personal and professional tools.
It is as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify were all available at the same time on a single browser.
The peculiarity of the online personal data store is that it is entirely the responsibility of the user to decide who can access what kind of information.
The company calls this “personal responsibility via data”.
According to John Bruce’s CEO, Inrupt’s idea is for the company to provide appropriate resources, processes,, and skills to make Solidarity accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, a few developers working on the project contracts, and a community of voluntary encoders.
Starting this week, technology developers from all over the world will be able to create their own decentralised applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee has argued that he and his team are not yet in dispute with “Facebook and Google” to determine whether or not to make a complete change where all their business models are completely upset in an instant.
“We don't ask them for permission.”
In an article published on Saturday on Medium, Berners-Lee writes that “the mission [of Inrupt) consists in providing commercial energy and an ecosystem with the aim of protecting the integrity and quality of the new web built on Solid.”
In 1994, the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Beners-Lee transformed the Internet into the World Wide Web Consortium.
Over the past few months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the cross-net neutrality debate.
Despite launching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I am extremely optimistic about this new era of the web,” added Berners-Lee.
Bernard Vann: Celebration of an Ecclesiastic of the First World Wrath Decorated with the Victoria Cross
The only ecclesiastic of the Church of England to receive the Victoria Cross during the First World LawWord was celebrated in his hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant-Colonel Bernard Vann won this distinction on September 29, 1918, during the attack in Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, he was shattered by a sniper four days later and did not know that he had obtained the highest British military distinction.
Its two grandsons unveiled a commemorative stone during a parade in Rushden in Northamptonshire last Saturday.
Michael Vann, one of his grandsons, claimed that it was "brilliantly symbolic" to unveil the stone exactly 100 years after his grandfather funeral.
According to the London Gazatte, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant-Colonel Vann steered his Battalion on the Saint-Quentin Canal “through a very thick mischagger and under the violent firing of field guns and machine-guns”.
Later he precipiously rushed to the firing line and, with the "greatest bravery", guided the line before blerupting a campaign cannon on his own and stunning three detachment soldiers.
On October 4, 1918, a German sniper killed Lieutenant-Colonel Vann, just over a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, age 72, stated that his grandfather's deeds were "a thing equal to which I will never be, but that makes me humble".
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also deposited a wreath after the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band parade.
Michael Vann stated that he felt "very honoured to be part of the parade" and added that "the courage of an authentic hero is reflected in the support that will be given to him by many people."
Fans of mixed martartial arts stayed woken up all night to watch Bellator 206.
Imagine this, you spent the whole night looking at a busy Bellator 206, but you couldn't see the main event.
In San Jose, the event included 13 fights, including six on the main sheet, and was broadcast live throughout the night in the United Kingdom on Channel 5.
At 06 a.m. when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to face each other, viewers from the United Kingdom were staggered when broadcasting changed to Peppa Pig.
Some were dissatisfied that they stayed awake until the early hours of the morning especially for the fight.
A tweater described the move to children's cartoons as "a kind of bad joke."
With regard to transmission, Bellator Senior Vice-President of Marketing and Communications Dave Schwartz stated that “the government regulation states that at 6 a.m., this content was unsuitable, so they had to switch to children's programmes”.
"Peppa the Pig," yes.
Scott Coker, Bellator’s president, has stated that they will work on their programming to include British viewers in the future.
“I believe that when I think about rebroadcasing, I think we can probably find a solution,” said Coker.
“However, it is six a.m. on a Sunday, and we will not be able to find a solution until Sunday, i.e. on Monday.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, when the program tilted, there were a lot of messages in both directions and they weren't all friendly.
We were trying to fix, we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn't a problem, it was a government issue.
I can assure you that this won't happen next time.
We will confine ourselves to five fights instead of six – as we usually do – and we have tried to donate more for the fans, and we ruined everything.
This is an unfortunate situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt "botlow" in terms of sexuality
Olympic diver Tom Daley says he grew up feeling inferior to the others because of his sexuality – but this infuriated his motivation to succeed.
The 24-year-old said he didn't realize until he went to high school that "everyone was like me."
As part of Lauren Laverne's first Radio 4, Desert Island Discs, he said that he had talked about the issue of gay rights to give "hope".
He also said that becoming a parent had made him less interested in winning the Olympics.
Kirsty Young’s regular presenter took off for several months because of her illness.
Appeared as a casvougregated in Laverne’s first show, Daley said he felt “less than” all the other children growing up because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to loving boys and girls.”
He said, “Stand again, these feelings of being inferior and different are the real things that have given me the power and strength for my success.”
In order not to disappoint everyone when they finally learned the truth about his sexuality, he wanted to prove that it was “something,” he continued.
The double Olympic bronze medallist is now a renowned LGBT activist and took advantage this year’s participation in the Commonwealth Games in Australia to call on more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said that he was speaking because he felt lucky to be able to live openly without its ramifications, so he wanted to restore “hunthow” to others.
The three-time world champion has said that he “doesn’t wait” to fall in love with a man – American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, who met in 2013.
Daley last year married Oscar laureate, 20-year-old his eldest, but he stated that the age difference had never been a problem.
“When we saw so much at such a young age” – he competed in his first Olympic Games at the age of 14, and his father died of cancer three years later – he said that it was difficult to find a person of the same age who experienced the same ups and down.
In June, they became relatives of a boy named Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley stated that "his whole perspective" had changed.
“Had you questioned me last year, I only thought about winning a gold medal,” he said.
“you know, there are more important things than the Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic gold medal is Robbie."
His son bears the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 as a result of brain cancer.
Daley confided that his father had not agreed that he was going to die and that one of the last things he asked him was whether they already had their tickets for London 2012, since he wanted to be seated in the first row.
"I couldn't tell him, 'Dad, you won't be here to be in the first row,'" he said.
“I was holding it by the hand when it stopped breathing, and it was necessary for him to stop breathing and he was dead to finally recognize that he wasn’t invincible,” he added.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won a bronze medal.
“This was all I had dreamed of all my life – drifting to a crowd at home during the Olympic Games, there is nothing better,” he said.
In addition, this inspired his first choice of song – Proud from Heather Small – which had accompanied him during the preparations for the Olympics and still gave him goosebump.
Desert Island Discs is on BBC Radio on Sunday at 11:15 a.m. BST.
An unfit Mickelson on Ryder Cup bench on Saturday
U.S. Phil Mickelson will set a record this Sunday by playing his 47th Ryder Cup Game, but he will have to stand straight to prevent it being an unhaired step.
Mickelson, who competed bi-yearly for the twelfth time, a record was set aside by captain Jim Furyk of Saturday's fourballs and foursome.
Instead of being at the heart of the course of the action, as he has so often been for the United States, the quintuple grand champion shared his day between the role of support and the embossing of his game at the booth in an attempt to remedy his ailments.
It has never been the greatest leader, even at the height of his quarantine. At the age of 48, it is not the most suitable for the difficult National Golf, where the long rough systematically punishes strunteal beating.
Mickelson, in the ninth game on Sunday, Mickelson faced Francesco Molinari, winner of the British Open, who teamed up with Tommy Fleetwood this week to win all four games.
If the Americans at the start of the 12 single matches make a good start, Mickelson’s match could be absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed his confidence in his man, but he couldn't say much else.
