According to a survey, the ideal time for a pint is 5.30 p.m. on Saturday.
Summer heatwave raised revenues from struggling British pubs, but increased the pressure on restaurant chains.
Figures indicate that groups of bass and pub grew by 2.7 per cent in their sales in July, but restaurant revenues fell by 4.8.
Peter Martin of the CGA consulting firm, who compiled the data, said: “The permanent sunshine and longer-to-expected participation in the World Cup meant that the month of July followed the month of the month of June, when bars had risen 2.8%, with the exception of restaurants, which suffered an even greater shock.
The 1.8 per cent drop in the catering sector in June only worsened in July.
Pubs and bars where alcoholic beverages served are by far the most popular, with more resemblances than declining restaurants.
Dietary pubs have also suffered the effects of the sun, but not as much as restaurateurs.
It seems that people just feel like going out for a drink.
In pubbs and sea bass, beverage sales increased by 6.6 per cent over the month and food fell by 3 per cent.”
Paul Newman, Leisure Analyst and RSM HighlyHietry Analyst, said: “These results are following the trend since the end of April.
Weather and the impact of major social or sporting events remain the most important factors in terms of sales in the out-of-home market.
It is not surprising that catering groups continue to experience difficulties, although an annual average decline of 4.8 per cent is particularly painful, in addition to the continuing cost pressures.
The long and hot summer could not have been less appropriate for farmers in the food sector, and the weather will tell us if the more moderate temperatures we experienced in August will give a well-deserved respite.”
The total increase in sales in pubs and restaurants, including new outlets, was 2.7% in July, reflecting the slowdown.
Industry sales are monitored by the Coffer Peach Tracker for the United Kingdom’s pubs, bass and restaurant sector, which collects and analyses the performance data of 47 operating groups with a combined turnover of more than Pound9 billion Sterling Pound and is the standard in the established industry.
