Earthquakes and Tsunshades in Indonesia
At least 3,884 people were killed by a powerful earthquake and tsunami that devastated the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, the authorities said, and the death toll is expected.
As communications were paralysed, the rescue services could not receive any information from the Donggala regency, an area north of Palu further close to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
More than 1 1 1 16,000 people were evacuated following the disaster.
Some essential facts about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi Province, located at the tip of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, with an estimated 339,800 population in 2017.
The city celebrated its 40th anniversary when the Earthquake and Tsunami took place.
Donggala is a regency running more than 300 km (180 mile) from the north-western coast of Sulawesi Island.
It is estimated that the regency population, an administrative region below a province, is estimated at 299,200 in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the main pillars of the Central Sulawesi province, especially the coastal region of Donggala.
N nickel extraction is also an important activity in the province, but it is mainly located in Morowali, on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been affected by Tsunami several times for a century, according to the Indonesian Disaster Reduction Agency.
In 1938, a Tsunami killed more than 200 lives and destroyed hundreds of residences in Donggala.
Tsunami also struck western Donggala in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia sits on the Pacific Seismic Fire Circle and is regularly shackled by earthquakes.
Here are some of the major earthquakes and sunsawns in recent years:
2004: A major earthquake occurred on 26 December on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh, unleashed a tsunami that sweeped over 14 countries, killing 226,000 people on the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half in Aceh.
2005: Several violent earthquakes shook the west coast of Sumatra from late March to the beginning of April.
A few hundred people died on Nias Island off Sumatra.
2006: An Earthquake of 6.8 reached southern Java, the most inhabited island in Indonesia, unleashing a tsunami that hit the south coast and claimed nearly 700 lives.
2009: An Earthquake with a magnitude of 7.6 shackled Padang City, the capital of West Sumatra Province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: An Earthquake with a magnitude of 7.5 devastated one of the Mentawai Islands off Sumatra, unleashed a tsunami of more than 10 metres that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A superficial earthquake shackled the regency of Pidie Jaya in Aceh, provoking destruction and panic, reminiscent of the devastation wrought caused by the 2004 bruised zequacy and tsunami.
No tsunami this time was caused, but more than 100 people were killed by the collapsing of buildings.
2018: Major earthquakes shook the Indonesian tourist island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
The Earthquake destroyed several thousand buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily blocked.
