Federal prosecutors in New York claim the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, the alleged New York assailant who claimed eight victims – a rare sentence that has not been applied for a federal felony since 1953.
Saipov, a 30-year-atillist, allegedly used a truck rented from Home Depot to tackle a bike lane on the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan, falsifying and a cyclist last October.
In order to justify a death sentence, prosecutors will need to demonstrate that Saipov “intentionally” assassinated the eight victims and “involuntarily inflicted” serious bodily harm, according to the statement of intent to seek the death penalty filed with the Southern District of New York.
These two charges may result in the death penalty, according to the judicial document.
A few weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury convicted Saipov of 22 counts, including eight indictments for extortion of extortion, usually used by federal prosecutors in organized criminality cases and one for crimes of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The Attack required “quitty preparation and premeditation,” the prosecutors said, calling Saipov’s “odorous, cruel and depraved method.”
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco,” says the statement of intent.
Five of the victims were Argentinian tourists.
The Southern District of New York had not demanded the death penalty for 10 years.
The accused, Khalid Barnes, was found guilty of the murder of two drug traffickers but was subsequently sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time the death penalty was applied in a New York federal case, in 1953 in the Julius and Ethel Rosenberg case, a married couple executed for conspiring and espionage for the USSR during the two-year Cold War.
Both Rosenbergs were executed by electric chair on June 19, 19, 1953.
Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, demonstrated a lack of remorse in the days and months following the tragedy, according to court records.
According to the police, he told the investigators that he was pleased with his actions.
Saipov told the authorities that he had been incited to commit the attack after starring Daesh videos on his phone, according to the indictment.
He also asked to display Daesh’s flapper in his hospital bedroom, the police said.
He pleaded non-guilty on 22 counts.
David Patton, one of Saipov’s federal public defenders, said he was “obviously disillusioned” with the decision to shoot.
“We believe that the decision to seek the death penalty rather than to accept his guilty plea and to sentence it to life imprisonment without the possibility of release can only prolong the trauma associated with these events for everyone concerned,” Patton said.
Saipov’s defence team had already asked prosecutors not to claim the death penalty.
