How US-Chinetize the US-Chinetize Trade War Could Benefit in Beijing
The first salvlies of US-Chinetized trade war were deafening, and although the battle is far from over, a disagreement between countries could benefit Beijing in the long run, according to experts.
U.S. President Donald Trump issued the first spawning warning earlier this year by taxing key Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel, and aluminum.
The largest increase occurred this week with new tariffs affecting USD 200 billion (Pound 150 billion), taxing one-half of all goods from China arriving in the United States.
Each time, Beijing resettled in kind, most recently striking tariffs of 5 to 10 per cent on 60-billion-dollar United States of United States.
China has pledged to match the United States, and it is unlikely that the world’s second-largest economy will falter soon.
Shifting back Washington means giving in to demands, but publicly yielding to the United States would be far too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, China’s president.
Operators believe, however, that if Beijing can play its part properly, the pressures of the US trade war could have a positive effect on China in the long term by reducing the interdependence of the two economies.
“The fact that a rapid political decision in Washington or Beijing can create conditions for an economic slowdown in one or other of the countries is in fact much more dangerous than what observers have recognized before,” said Abigail Grace, a research associate that focuses on Asia at the Center for New American Security’s Think Tank.
“Prepared” for refugee return, Foreign Minister says
Syria declares its readiness for the voluntary return of the refugees and is seeking assistance to rebuild this country, which has been devastated by more than seven-year-old war.
Addressing the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said that the situation in the country was improving.
“Today, the situation on the ground is more stable and safer thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism,” he said.
The Government continues to rehabilitate areas destroyed by the terrorists in order to restore normalcy.
All the conditions are now in place for the voluntary repatriation of the refugees to the country whom they have been forced to leave because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures aimed at their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.50 million Syrians have deserted the country since the beginning of the war in 2011.
An additional six million people still living in the country are in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem stated that the Syrian regime would welcome assistance for the reconstruction of this devastated country.
He stressed, however, that it would not accept conditional assistance or assistance from countries sponsoring the insurgency.
Europe win Ryder Cup in Paris
The European team won the Ryder Cup 2018 by beating the United States team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at the Golf National near Paris, France.
The United States has now lost six consecutive times on European soil and has not won a Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe reclaimed the title when Danish captain Thomas Bjorn’s team crossed the 14.5-point mark needed to defeat the United States.
U.S. star Phil Mickelson, who was in trouble for most of the tournament, smashed his kick into the water at 16 hole par-3, ceding his duel to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari excelled in all his rounds, becoming one of the four players ever to reach 5–0–0 from the current tournament format, whose first edition dates back to 1979.
Jordan Spieth was discarded 5–4 by the least well-placed European player, Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
Best golfer in the world, Dustin Johnson, tilted 2 and 1 against Ian Poulter, who might have played his last Ryder Cup.
Spaniard Sergio Garcia became an all-time European champion with a total of 25.5 career points.
